  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Alan H. Guth",
          "Andrei Linde",
          "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
        ],
        "citation": "for the theory of cosmic inflation and the quantum origin of cosmological structure"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B038"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B003 remains the most profound shortlisted account linking a physical early-universe mechanism to the origin of the observable large-scale structure. Inflation supplied the dynamical idea, viable inflationary realizations made it a durable framework, and the quantum-perturbation account connected it to the CMB and structure observations. Guth, Linde, and Mukhanov correspond to these distinct indispensable steps. The case rests on that empirical legacy, not on theoretical elegance alone.",
    "case_against": "Present observations support important broad expectations of inflation and primordial quantum perturbations, but do not select a unique inflationary mechanism or realization over all competing early-universe accounts. Moreover, the theoretical lineage extends beyond three people. These are serious limits on both maturity and attribution, and the citation must not claim a uniquely established microscopic account.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce argues that the gamma-ray-burst afterglow discovery is a bounded observational transition, whereas B003 has non-unique mechanisms and a broad theoretical lineage.",
        "response": "The non-uniqueness concern is decisive for how modestly B003 must be framed, but not sufficient to remove it from preference. Inflation with quantum primordial perturbations changed cosmology by offering a testable account of the observed cosmic pattern. B030 is an important alternative, yet its own discovery chain runs through instrument teams and a broad follow-up community, leaving its three-person allocation no cleaner without the requested historical review."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll maintains that B013 has directly testable 10^-18 performance, compact Katori-Ye attribution, and a more settled prize boundary than inflation.",
        "response": "I agree that B013 is exceptionally mature experimentally and that its attribution is presently the clearest among the leading alternatives. It is therefore now my first alternative. Still, a precision architecture should be judged on the demonstrated physical advance rather than anticipated applications, and its historical boundary between Katori's original concept and later performance advances remains to be fixed. B003 retains greater explanatory reach, provided that we do not overstate what observations establish."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson treats B038 as far-reaching but questions whether the Aharonov-Bohm and Berry effects can properly be joined in one citation because of their separate origins.",
        "response": "I continue to regard both effects as profound and experimentally established, but I accept that the joint formulation is a substantive historical problem rather than a matter of wording. That makes B038 a strong alternative only if its unity can be defended. By comparison, B003 has a continuous physical narrative from early-universe dynamics through quantum perturbations to observable structure, even though its attribution and mechanism require continued scrutiny."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How strongly the available observational record distinguishes inflation and its quantum-perturbation account from sufficiently broad competing early-universe explanations.",
      "Whether Guth, Linde, and Mukhanov are the historically most defensible three-person representation of the full development, and whether the citation can remain accurate without implying a unique microscopic mechanism."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
